{%- comment -%}
  meta-tags.liquid  —  REVISED
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Drop-in replacement for the live theme's snippets/meta-tags.liquid
  (theme "Copy of GS-style PDP build - Used Sep 17 - 20th",
   gid://shopify/OnlineStoreTheme/145734172723, 9336 bytes as pulled 2026-09-24).

  WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY — read this before diffing.

  1. HOMEPAGE ENTITY MARKUP MOVED OUT.
     The inline Organization and WebSite blocks are gone; this now renders
     `brand-organization`. The old Organization block carried four properties
     (name, url, logo, sameAs) which is not enough for Google to resolve a
     brand entity, and its sameAs array emitted every one of the six social
     theme settings unconditionally — so any unset setting arrived at Google
     as an empty string inside the array. The replacement filters blanks and
     adds legalName, address, contactPoint, description, foundingDate,
     alternateName and a return policy.

  2. PRODUCT SCHEMA: OFFERS NO LONGER TRUNCATE.
     The old block emitted one Offer per variant with `limit: 50`. Products in
     this catalogue carry up to 168 variants, so offers were being silently cut
     off. This version emits a single Offer when every variant shares one price
     (the common case here) and an AggregateOffer with a real offerCount when
     they differ. Nothing is dropped either way.

  3. PRODUCT SCHEMA: BRAND IS NOW A REFERENCE, NOT A RAW STRING.
     Was `"brand": {"@type":"Brand","name": product.vendor}`. The vendor field
     is fragmented across the catalogue — `Celeste L’Amour`, `CELESTELAMOUR`
     and `Celeste L'Amour` all appear — so this was publishing three different
     brands. It now points at the single `#brand` node declared on the
     homepage, which makes every product resolve to one entity immediately,
     before the vendor bulk-edit is finished.

  4. PRODUCT SCHEMA: ADDED itemCondition, priceValidUntil, shippingDetails,
     hasMerchantReturnPolicy, multi-image array and @id.
     These are the properties behind the "Free delivery", "Free returns" and
     price annotations that merchant listings show. Values come from the
     store's own published Shipping and Refund policies, not from guesses.

  5. AGGREGATERATING IS NOW GUARDED HARDER — see the long comment at that block.

  6. SKU IS NOW SUPPRESSED WHEN IT IS A SUPPLIER STRING.
     Many variants carry SKUs like `191288010:-1#Pink;191288664:28313#Xs`.
     That is a supplier-side identifier, not a retail SKU; publishing it in
     structured data tells anyone reading the markup exactly which dropship
     catalogue the product came from. An absent sku is better than that one.

  UNCHANGED: the title/description/OG/Twitter logic, and the current_tags
  handling with its comment about not piping a filter onto a translation
  argument — that was already correct.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign share_title = page_title
  assign share_kind = 'website'
  assign share_url = canonical_url
  assign share_summary = page_description | default: shop.description | default: shop.name
  assign share_media = blank

  case request.page_type
    when 'product'
      assign share_kind = 'product'
      if product.featured_media
        assign share_media = product.featured_media.preview_image
      endif
    when 'article'
      assign share_kind = 'article'
      if article.image
        assign share_media = article.image
      endif
    when 'collection'
      if collection.image
        assign share_media = collection.image
      endif
    when 'password'
      assign share_url = shop.url
  endcase

  if share_media == blank and settings.share_image
    assign share_media = settings.share_image
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  current_tags is joined into its own variable before it ever reaches a `t:`
  call. Piping a filter directly onto a translation argument
  (`t: tags: current_tags | join: ', '`) filters the translated string
  instead of the argument that was meant to feed it — exactly the kind of
  construct a strict Liquid parser flags on upload.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if current_tags -%}
  {%- assign tag_summary = current_tags | join: ', ' -%}
{%- endif -%}

<title>
  {{- page_title -}}
  {%- if current_tags %} | {{ 'general.meta.tags' | t: tags: tag_summary }}{% endif -%}
  {%- if current_page != 1 %} | {{ 'general.meta.page' | t: page: current_page }}{% endif -%}
  {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} | {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
</title>

{%- comment -%}
  Meta description now falls back to the store-level description instead of
  emitting nothing. Set that value at Online Store → Preferences; it is
  currently null, which is why the homepage has no controlled snippet.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign meta_description = page_description | default: shop.description -%}
{%- if meta_description != blank -%}
  <meta name="description" content="{{ meta_description | escape }}">
{%- endif -%}

<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ shop.name }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ share_kind }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ share_url }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ share_title | escape }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ share_summary | escape }}">

{%- if share_media != blank -%}
  <meta property="og:image" content="https:{{ share_media | image_url: width: 1200 }}">
  <meta property="og:image:secure_url" content="https:{{ share_media | image_url: width: 1200 }}">
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="{{ 1200 | divided_by: share_media.aspect_ratio | round }}">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ share_media.alt | escape }}">
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'product' -%}
  <meta property="og:price:amount" content="{{ product.price | divided_by: 100.0 }}">
  <meta property="og:price:currency" content="{{ cart.currency.iso_code }}">
{%- endif -%}

<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ share_title | escape }}">
<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ share_summary | escape }}">

{%- comment -%} ---------- Structured data ---------- {%- endcomment -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'index' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Organization + WebSite live in their own snippet now. Declared once, on the
    homepage only — Google expects a single Organization entity per site.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'brand-organization' %}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'product' -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign org_id = shop.url | append: '/#organization'
    assign brand_id = shop.url | append: '/#brand'
    assign return_id = shop.url | append: '/#returnpolicy'
    assign product_abs_url = shop.url | append: product.url

    assign rating_value = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value.rating
    assign rating_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | plus: 0

    # A supplier SKU looks like `191288010:-1#Pink;191288664:28313#Xs`.
    # Treat anything containing a colon or a hash as not a retail identifier.
    assign raw_sku = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.sku
    assign sku_is_clean = true
    if raw_sku == blank
      assign sku_is_clean = false
    elsif raw_sku contains ':' or raw_sku contains '#'
      assign sku_is_clean = false
    endif

    # priceValidUntil is required for some merchant-listing treatments. One
    # year out is the conventional value for an always-on catalogue price.
    assign price_valid_until = 'now' | date: '%Y' | plus: 1
    assign price_valid_until = price_valid_until | append: '-12-31'

    assign single_price = false
    if product.price_min == product.price_max
      assign single_price = true
    endif
  -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org/",
      "@type": "Product",
      "@id": {{ product_abs_url | append: '#product' | json }},
      "name": {{ product.title | strip_html | json }},
      "url": {{ product_abs_url | json }},
      "description": {{ product.description | strip_html | strip_newlines | truncate: 5000 | json }},
      {%- if product.media.size > 0 -%}
      "image": [
        {%- for m in product.media limit: 6 -%}
          {{ m | image_url: width: 1920 | prepend: 'https:' | json }}{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      ],
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if sku_is_clean -%}
      "sku": {{ raw_sku | json }},
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if product.type != blank -%}
      "category": {{ product.type | json }},
      {%- endif -%}
      "brand": { "@id": {{ brand_id | json }} },

      {%- comment -%}
        AGGREGATERATING — READ BEFORE ENABLING.

        This reads `product.metafields.reviews.rating`, the Shopify-native
        review namespace. It deliberately does NOT read `judgeme.*`.

        Judge.me currently reports 142 reviews store-wide at 4.69, and at least
        one product (7830848110643) carries a Judge.me badge advertising 100
        reviews at 4.60 whose bodies read, verbatim:
          "DEMO / TEST REVIEW — NOT FOR PUBLICATION. Layout test scenario 100…"
        under reviewer names like "DEMO S.B." and "DEMO A.P.", all with
        verified_buyer: false.

        Publishing those as a rating rich result would be a fabricated review
        snippet. That is a structured-data manual action under Google's spam
        policies, and for a US seller it is also exposure under the FTC Rule on
        Consumer Reviews and Testimonials (16 CFR Part 465), which took effect
        in October 2024 and reaches fake and unsubstantiated consumer reviews.

        Before wiring any review app into this block: delete every DEMO row in
        Judge.me, confirm the remaining count is real, and confirm the reviews
        are actually visible on the product page — Google requires that the
        marked-up rating be present in the page's visible content.

        Also check Judge.me's own settings. It can inject its own rich-snippet
        markup independently of the theme, which would bypass this guard
        entirely. Judge.me → Settings → SEO / Rich snippets.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if rating_value != blank and rating_count > 0 -%}
      "aggregateRating": {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        "ratingValue": {{ rating_value | json }},
        "bestRating": 5,
        "worstRating": 1,
        "reviewCount": {{ rating_count }}
      },
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Offers. Single Offer when every variant is the same price, which is the
        common case in this catalogue; AggregateOffer otherwise. The previous
        version emitted one Offer per variant capped at 50, which silently
        truncated products carrying 102 and 168 variants.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "offers": {
        {%- if single_price -%}
        "@type": "Offer",
        "price": {{ product.price_min | divided_by: 100.0 }},
        {%- else -%}
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        "lowPrice": {{ product.price_min | divided_by: 100.0 }},
        "highPrice": {{ product.price_max | divided_by: 100.0 }},
        "offerCount": {{ product.variants.size }},
        {%- endif -%}
        "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
        "priceValidUntil": {{ price_valid_until | json }},
        "itemCondition": "https://schema.org/NewCondition",
        "availability": {% if product.available %}"https://schema.org/InStock"{% else %}"https://schema.org/OutOfStock"{% endif %},
        "url": {{ product_abs_url | json }},
        "seller": { "@id": {{ org_id | json }} },
        "hasMerchantReturnPolicy": { "@id": {{ return_id | json }} },

        {%- comment -%}
          Shipping figures are taken from the store's published Shipping policy:
          1–3 business days handling, Standard 3–5 business days at $7.99, free
          Standard on orders of $75 or more. If the policy changes, change this
          too — shipping markup that contradicts the policy page is worse than
          none, because Google cross-checks it and can suppress the annotation
          for the whole domain.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        "shippingDetails": {
          "@type": "OfferShippingDetails",
          "shippingRate": {
            "@type": "MonetaryAmount",
            "value": 7.99,
            "currency": "USD"
          },
          "shippingDestination": {
            "@type": "DefinedRegion",
            "addressCountry": "US"
          },
          "deliveryTime": {
            "@type": "ShippingDeliveryTime",
            "handlingTime": {
              "@type": "QuantitativeValue",
              "minValue": 1,
              "maxValue": 3,
              "unitCode": "DAY"
            },
            "transitTime": {
              "@type": "QuantitativeValue",
              "minValue": 3,
              "maxValue": 5,
              "unitCode": "DAY"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'article' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Article markup is retained, but note what it is currently doing: the blog
    at /blogs/verbs-list holds 57 scraped third-party articles (PrepScholar,
    Ivy Coach, College Transitions and similar), and this block stamps every
    one of them with `"publisher": Celeste L'Amour`. That is the brand
    formally claiming authorship of plagiarised content, to Google, in
    machine-readable form.

    Removing this markup does not fix that. Removing the articles does.
    See ../../google/worklist-blog-cleanup.md.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "Article",
      "@id": {{ shop.url | append: article.url | append: '#article' | json }},
      "headline": {{ article.title | json }},
      "articleBody": {{ article.content | strip_html | json }},
      {% if article.image -%}
        "image": [{{ article.image | image_url: width: 1920 | prepend: "https:" | json }}],
      {%- endif %}
      "datePublished": {{ article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ' | json }},
      "dateModified": {{ article.updated_at | default: article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ' | json }},
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ article.author | json }} },
      "publisher": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#organization' | json }} },
      "isPartOf": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#website' | json }} },
      "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": {{ shop.url | append: article.url | json }} }
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'collection' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    NEW. Collection pages previously emitted only a BreadcrumbList. A
    CollectionPage node with an ItemList gives Google an explicit, named,
    navigational entity for each category — which is the raw material it draws
    on when choosing sitelinks. This is the markup side of the sitelinks work;
    the other half is main-nav placement and real collection descriptions.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "CollectionPage",
      "@id": {{ shop.url | append: collection.url | append: '#collection' | json }},
      "url": {{ shop.url | append: collection.url | json }},
      "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
      {%- if collection.description != blank -%}
      "description": {{ collection.description | strip_html | strip_newlines | truncate: 500 | json }},
      {%- endif -%}
      "isPartOf": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#website' | json }} },
      "about": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#brand' | json }} },
      "mainEntity": {
        "@type": "ItemList",
        "numberOfItems": {{ collection.products_count }},
        "itemListElement": [
          {%- for p in collection.products limit: 12 -%}
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": {{ forloop.index }},
            "url": {{ shop.url | append: p.url | json }},
            "name": {{ p.title | strip_html | json }}
          }{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        ]
      }
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'product' or request.page_type == 'collection' or request.page_type == 'article' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    BreadcrumbList. Unchanged in shape, but products now route through their
    primary collection so the trail is Home → Category → Product rather than
    Home → Product. A two-step breadcrumb tells Google nothing about site
    structure; a three-step one is part of how it infers the category pages
    that deserve to be sitelinks.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign crumb_collection = blank
    if request.page_type == 'product'
      assign crumb_collection = product.metafields.custom.category_collection.value
      if crumb_collection == blank
        assign crumb_collection = collection
      endif
      if crumb_collection == blank
        assign crumb_collection = product.collections.first
      endif
    endif
  -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": {{ 'general.breadcrumbs.home' | t | json }},
          "item": {{ shop.url | json }}
        }
        {%- if request.page_type == 'product' -%}
          {%- if crumb_collection != blank -%},
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": {{ crumb_collection.title | json }},
            "item": {{ shop.url | append: crumb_collection.url | json }}
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": {{ product.title | json }},
            "item": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }}
          }
          {%- else -%},
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": {{ product.title | json }},
            "item": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }}
          }
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- elsif request.page_type == 'collection' -%},
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
            "item": {{ shop.url | append: collection.url | json }}
          }
        {%- elsif request.page_type == 'article' -%},
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": {{ blog.title | json }},
            "item": {{ shop.url | append: blog.url | json }}
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": {{ article.title | json }},
            "item": {{ shop.url | append: article.url | json }}
          }
        {%- endif -%}
      ]
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
